{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0161ZeTcKXnw5JQm2s7NLYMV","type":"tool_result","content":"VERSION ?= $(shell git describe --tags --always --dirty 2>/dev/null || echo dev)\nLDFLAGS := -s -w -X main.version=$(VERSION)\n\n.PHONY: build test lint fmt clean cve-fixtures chart-test image-test\n\nbuild:\n\tCGO_ENABLED=0 go build -ldflags \"$(LDFLAGS)\" -o bin/ard ./cmd/ard\n\ntest:\n\tgo test ./...\n\nlint:\n\tgo vet ./...\n\t@out=\"$$(gofmt -l .)\"; if [ -n \"$$out\" ]; then echo \"gofmt needed:\"; echo \"$$out\"; exit 1; fi\n\nfmt:\n\tgofmt -w .\n\n# Renders the two Argo CD charts under deploy/ and checks them (needs helm and yq;\n# run by the ard-ci pipeline, not part of `make test`).\nchart-test:\n\tdeploy/test-charts.sh\n\n# Builds the ard image for both platforms and checks it (needs Docker buildx and\n# network; not part of `make test`).\nimage-test:\n\tdeploy/test-image.sh\n\n# Regenerates the real cve scanner fixtures inside the pinned tool image.\n# Needs Docker and network; not part of `make test`.\ncve-fixtures:\n\tdocker build -t ard-cve-fixtures internal/roles/scanners/cve\n\tdocker run --rm --user \"$$(id -u):$$(id -g)\" -e HOME=/tmp \\\n\t  -v \"$$PWD/internal/roles:/roles\" -w /roles ard-cve-fixtures sh testdata/cve/regen.sh\n\nclean:\n\trm -rf bin\n66:   ./bin/ard serve --repo albertcbrown/ard-scratch 2>&1 | tee -a ~/.ard/gate2-scratch.log\n69:   `serve` creates its bare clone under `~/.ard/repos/` on first start.\n83:   **Squash and merge**. Stop `serve` with Ctrl-C (SIGINT); it stops cleanly\n88:signal, restarting `serve` when no job is running (`SELECT COUNT(*) FROM jobs\n132:4. **Run** `serve` as in stage A with `--repo albertcbrown/ard`, in tmux or\n85:Four more labels sit outside the grammar: `ard:approved` (human go-ahead before a worker starts; counts only when a triage-or-higher collaborator added it after triage finished), `ard:tests:failed` (worker could not get tests green; set by worker close-out on the issue and the PR, cleared by a green run), `ard:keep` (hold failed-job resources past 24 h) and `ard:pause` (on the pinned status issue, stops all new claims). `ard labels sync` creates and updates the full set from the resolved config, idempotently, and never deletes a label: stale `ard:` labels are only reported. In `label` mode a custom `intake.label` is part of the catalogue: sync creates it with a description saying it admits the issue to triage, and switching back to the default leaves the old custom label reported as stale, never deleted. `ard labels sync` and `ard config check` reject an intake label that is one of ard's own label names, naming `intake.label`; `ard serve` logs `config.intake_invalid` once per distinct value. `--repo owner/name` names the repository; when omitted, it is read from the `origin` remote. Role names are at most 31 characters and scanner names at most 38, because GitHub caps a label at 50 characters. Worker role names may not contain an all-digit segment (`eng-12`), so branch names stay unambiguous.\n184:**Each scanner has its own state, and GitHub is the truth.** Every scanner keeps a separate SQLite file, `~/.ard/state/<owner>/<repo>/<scanner>.db`: fingerprint to issue, first and last seen, suppressions, last run. Scanners never share or lock each other's state, and one can be reset without touching the rest. The controller keeps its own jobs, attempts, events and a small key/value table (for example the status issue number) in `_controller.db` in the same directory, and dry-run uses `~/.ard/state-dryrun/<owner>/<repo>/`. Owner and repo are lowercased (GitHub names are case-insensitive) and nested, so distinct repos never share a directory (`a-b/c` and `a/b-c` would collide as `a-b-c`); `ard serve` and `ard rebuild-state` refuse to start while a pre-#82 flat `<owner>-<repo>` state directory exists under `state/` or `state-dryrun/`, and ard does not migrate it (decided, #82). Every issue body carries hidden markers, so `ard rebuild-state <scanner>` restores a database from GitHub after a lost disk or a host move. The body holds a header `<!-- ard:scanner=<name> group=<enc> -->` and a checklist between `<!-- ard:findings -->` and `<!-- /ard:findings -->`, one line per finding: `- [ ] <title> <!-- ard:fp=<enc> sev=<severity> [reg=<old issue>] -->`. `<enc>` percent-encodes every byte outside `[A-Za-z0-9._:/@+=,~-]`, so the proposal's example fingerprint stays readable and nothing can close the comment. A ticked box means the finding is gone. Per-finding details sit in a second span, `<!-- ard:details -->`, cut to 2,000 characters each and trimmed from the end to keep the body under 60,000; `issue.md` holds them untruncated. The controller reads and rewrites only the two spans, so text a human or triage adds elsewhere survives. A human who deletes a marker line makes that finding look new, which is accepted because GitHub is the truth.\n186:`ard rebuild-state <scanner>` takes the leader lock (it refuses to run while `ard serve` holds it), reads every issue with `ard:scanner:<name>`, open and closed, and replaces the scanner's findings and suppressions in one transaction, keeping the run history. For each fingerprint, an open issue wins (the lowest number), else the highest-numbered closed issue decides: completed leaves a `fixed` tombstone, not planned or duplicate leaves none. Independently, the highest-numbered not-planned or duplicate close gives a suppression until `closed_at + stale.suppress_for`, kept even when expired. It prints what it adds, removes and changes; with `--dry-run` it prints and writes nothing. The dry-run state database holds synthetic issues that are not on GitHub, so it is never rebuilt. Only scanners with a `scan` executable can be rebuilt. One known gap: reopening an old issue that shares a fingerprint with a newer one can leave the database different from a rebuild until the next rebuild-state.\n254:Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands; fetches, commits and pushes do not take it. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor in a pod, and the deterministic cve scanner runs there as a host subprocess of the controller container: its role directory has no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod.\n269:**Agent CLIs plug in through adapters.** Each adapter turns (prompt file, context paths, budget) into a command line and parses the exit code and, where the CLI reports them, turns and cost. For Claude Code that is `claude -p --output-format=stream-json --verbose --restricted --safe-mode --strict-mcp-config --disable-slash-commands --permission-mode=dontAsk --settings='{\"includeGitInstructions\":false,\"disableAllHooks\":true}' --tools=… --allowed-tools=… --disallowed-tools=… --model <model> --max-budget-usd <max_budget_usd> --append-system-prompt-file <role prompt> -- <message>` (`--verbose` is required by `stream-json` in print mode). Every stage (scanner, triage, worker, review) gets the same tool profile: `--tools` and `--allowed-tools` are `Read,Glob,Grep,Edit,Write,Bash`, and `--disallowed-tools` is `Bash(git commit *),Bash(git push *),Bash(git remote *),Bash(gh *)` (decided, #79). ard does not pass `--bare`: it limits the CLI to Bash, Read and Edit, and `--tools` cannot widen it (#105). The planted-config isolation `--bare` gave comes instead from `--restricted` (only managed settings and `--settings` load), `--safe-mode` (no CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md, skills, agents, hooks, MCP servers or auto memory), `--strict-mcp-config`, `--disable-slash-commands`, and `--settings` that turns off hooks and the git status snapshot (commit subjects are third-party text). `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_BACKGROUND_TASKS=1` is set as a fixed env var (the executor allows it) so Bash cannot detach commands. The integration tests pin each of these against a worktree with planted config and must be re-run on every `TestedVersion` bump. The tool list is not a security boundary, and the deny list is advisory, because shell indirection gets around it. The real boundaries are the missing GitHub token (Docker; see the host-mode note above, #84), the committer, which commits only `.ard/<issue#>/` from a triage or review job (other edits are discarded with the worktree), and the diff guard on worker changes. An explicit tool list from the caller (`Spec.Tools`) replaces the allow list and keeps the deny list. The user message holds only fixed text and the paths of the untrusted input files. The installed CLI accepts an undocumented `--max-turns`; ard does not rely on it, so a per-job dollar cap and the executor's hard timeout bound each run. Agents run with an empty per-job CLI config dir and HOME and receive exactly one model credential, passed by name by the executor and never visible to the adapter: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) or a subscription OAuth token (`CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`, from `claude setup-token`); that rests on the environment, not on a CLI flag. `ard serve` chooses the credential from its own environment: `ARD_AGENT_AUTH` is `auto` (the default; the token if set, else the key), `oauth` or `apikey`, and an explicit mode never falls back to the other variable. Only one is ever passed, because the CLI prefers the API key and would silently bill it (pinned by an integration test). A missing credential does not stop startup, because deterministic scanners need none: `ard serve` logs one INFO line, and a job that needs one fails with a clear error. The executor creates the per-job config dir as `<job home>/.claude` (0700) in `prepare` (`Handle.View.ConfigDir`; under Docker it is a bind mount at `/ard-config`), refuses to start a job whose config dir is not that empty directory, and removes it with the job home; the Reaper's `KeepWorktree` retention keeps it too, and it holds no credential, since the credential travels only in the environment. `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBPROCESS_ENV_SCRUB=1`, which would keep the token out of Bash children, is not set (decided, #125). Residual risk: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) is inherited by Bash children; on the tested release the CLI does not pass `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` to them; and either credential can still be read from the `claude` process's own environment (`/proc/$PPID/environ` on Linux, `ps eww` on macOS). The container is the boundary: it holds no GitHub token, and the credential is scoped to the model. The scrub is off because on 2.1.328 it requires bubblewrap (and socat) plus unprivileged user namespaces; Docker blocks those under ard's flags (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, default seccomp), so every Bash call would fail closed and without bwrap the CLI refuses to start. On macOS host mode it worked and matched the `dontAsk` outcomes. It can be revisited for host mode or a loosened container. What agent Bash can read differs by mode: under Docker, only the job's own credential; in host mode, far more, because any same-uid process's environment is readable (`ps eww <pid>` on macOS, `/proc/<pid>/environ` on Linux), including `ard serve`'s own, which holds both credentials. The scrub would not close that; it is part of the host-mode risk of #84. `kimi` and `dsh-tui` both run headless with `-p`; neither has a turn cap and `kimi` reports no cost, so turns and cost are optional in the adapter result. `config.yaml` names the default CLI and any per-role override, so a role can move between CLIs without touching its prompt. The Claude adapter also reads a usage limit and token usage from the stream: a limit comes from a `rate_limit_event` with status `rejected` (its `resetsAt` is the reset time), from the CLI's own error text (`You've hit your session, weekly, Opus or Sonnet limit`) and from `rate_limit` or `billing_error` errors, and it records `usage` and `modelUsage` token counts (by input, output, cache write and cache read; per model when the result has `modelUsage`, which is preferred for accounting). That text is matched only on paths the CLI itself writes (an assistant message carrying an `error`, or an errored result with HTTP 429 or `api_error`), never in agent output, because an agent can be made to print the phrase. With an API key a 429 stays a transient `rate_limit` retry; only subscription (OAuth) runs have usage limits. The committed fixtures are scrubbed real captures where a human has made them (`testdata/CAPTURE.md`) and clearly named `synthetic_*` files elsewhere.\n350:Configuration is by convention: a role exists because its directory exists, and `.ard/config.yaml` holds only global settings and overrides. No prompt path is written anywhere; ard reads `.ard/<type>/<name>/prompt.txt`. Model credentials are host settings (`ARD_AGENT_AUTH`, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` in the `ard serve` environment), not `config.yaml` keys, because a PR can change `config.yaml`.\n400:**Where `ard serve` reads config (decided, #87).** After each fetch into its own clone (`~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git`, see Local-executor details) `ard serve` resolves `origin/main` to a SHA and reads `config.yaml` and the role directories from that commit, not from the working tree. It exports them into a snapshot at `~/.ard/config/<owner>/<repo>/<sha>/`, kept 24 hours after a newer SHA replaces it, because roles run from a host path: a role directory, its `Dockerfile` and the scan worktree must all describe the one commit the tick's jobs use. Editing the host's working tree therefore changes nothing on a running controller, with one exception: a local `paused: true` is honoured (see the kill switch), and only that key is read locally. A merged change applies on the next tick without a pull. Symlinks and submodules under the role directories are rejected, and a config that cannot be fetched or does not validate counts as paused. If `origin/main` cannot be loaded at startup, `ard serve` starts paused instead of exiting, posts the load error to the status issue once per distinct error, and retries every tick; no loop runs until a config has loaded. `ard config check` validates the working tree, for authoring; `ard config check --ref <ref>` validates a commit of the local checkout (for example `--ref origin/main` after `git fetch`) without fetching.\n409:- **Intake is configurable (decided, #2).** `intake` is either `all` (every human-filed issue enters, no label) or `label` (only issues with the configured label). Issue templates and forms apply their labels on the author's behalf, so an outsider can file an issue that already carries the intake label. That is why, in label mode, the controller honours the label only if the latest `labeled` event came from an actor with triage or higher permission, or from the App; otherwise it adds `ard:needs-human`. The intake label must start with `ard:`, and a label ard itself applies (`ard:needs-human`, a stage label and so on) cannot be the intake label; `ard config check` and `ard labels sync` reject it, and `ard serve` never admits through it. In both modes the issue is re-gated if its body is edited afterwards. Note that `all` mode lets any issue author start a job, so use it only on repos where that is acceptable.\n411:- **Planted repo config does not steer agents.** A branch can carry CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, `.claude/` settings, hooks, skills, agents or a `.mcp.json`. The Claude adapter's flags stop all of them loading (see Executors). Residual risk: auth isolation comes from the empty per-job config dir and HOME, not a flag (the CLI reads no Keychain entry but the one keyed to the per-job dir); a host-mode job on macOS still runs as the operator's uid, so agent Bash could call `security` against the operator's login Keychain, including Claude's own entry, which is within the host-mode risk of #84 (a Dockerfile role or a dedicated macOS user for `ard serve` closes it); the subscription token is a one-year, model-only credential, rotated by running `claude setup-token` again and restarting `ard serve`; `--max-budget-usd` and `total_cost_usd` are estimates on a subscription; part of the block (AGENTS.md, and CLAUDE.md under `--restricted`) is observed behaviour that holds only on the tested CLI version and is re-checked by the integration tests; built-in plugins and the host's managed settings still apply; system reminders and fixed CLI prompt text are back; isolation of settings `env` is not pinned by a test, since no Bash call is scripted; Bash can still read anything the process can (host mode, #84); and an agent can rewrite CLAUDE.md or `.claude/` in its worktree, which later runs ignore but a worker PR could carry into the target repo (the `ard init` protected-path defaults cover `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `.claude/` and `.mcp.json`, so such a PR needs a human).\n420:- **Kill switch.** Four sources stop new claims, scanner runs (filing and autoclose), stale closes and merges: a local pause file `~/.ard/pause/<owner>/<repo>` outside the repository (the path is lowercased; `ard serve` creates the directory, so `touch` pauses and `rm` resumes), `paused: true` in the `config.yaml` in the host's working tree, `ard:pause` on the pinned status issue, and `paused: true` in the config on `origin/main`. They are checked cheapest first, before every claiming tick. The delay is at most one loop interval plus 10% jitter; a tick already running may finish its claims. The gate answers one controller-wide question and has no per-kind checks, so every loop that writes (scanner filing and autoclose, stale closes, merges, claims), except the Reaper and in-flight close-outs, must report `Claims() == true` and is skipped while paused. Running jobs, in-flight close-outs and the Reaper continue. The check fails closed: an unreadable status issue, a failed fetch of `origin/main`, or a config that fails to load counts as paused. The pause file pauses when anything exists at the path (a file, a directory or a symlink; content is ignored), and a path that cannot be examined, for example because `pause/<owner>` is a regular file, also counts as paused. The local working-tree `config.yaml` is read only for its `paused` key; a missing, unreadable or invalid file does not pause by itself, but a readable one that says `paused: true` does. The pause file and the local `paused` are the kill switches that nothing pushed to GitHub can lift. Config and role directories otherwise come from `origin/main` at the same SHA the tick's jobs use (see Configuration). `ard serve` remembers the status issue number in `_controller.db` (`meta` key `status_issue`). At startup it reads that issue directly and adopts it if it is still open, titled `ard status`, carries the `<!-- ard:status -->` marker and has the App as author; otherwise it searches by those same tests, and creates and pins the issue if none matches. Search is eventually consistent, so the remembered number is what stops a quick restart from creating a second issue; if two still match, the lowest number wins. A cached issue that cannot be read for a reason other than not-found falls back to search rather than aborting startup. A dry-run never stores its synthetic (negative) number; a real number found by search is stored only in the separate dry-run database. A second `ard serve` on the same repo exits with an error: the leader lock is an `flock` on `~/.ard/locks/<owner>/<repo>.lock` (local filesystem only), shared by live and dry-run.\n451:- **Kill switch in a cluster.** `kubectl -n <namespace> exec deploy/ard-<instance> -- touch /home/ard/.ard/pause/<owner>/<repo>` pauses `ard serve`; `rm` resumes.\n464:**Where it runs (decided, #5; amended for the soak, [#144](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/144)).** `ard serve` runs live on the owner's Mac with the local executor until Phase 3, when it moves to the \"spark\" Kubernetes cluster with the k8s executor and a Lease lock in place of the `flock`. The Gate 1 soak week does not run on the Mac: it runs on spark as `ard serve --dry-run` (one pod, `Recreate`, state on a persistent volume), and the week counts from the first healthy pod; no other `ard serve` for the same repo runs meanwhile, so one ledger is counted. See Delivery. ard supports macOS and Linux only; Windows is unsupported.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a29f340e-479a-455e-a288-951a76dc3dfc","uuid":"d95a225c-5d54-4928-9da1-7c0ac3b81d17","timestamp":"2026-11-23T12:15:13.007Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"VERSION ?= $(shell git describe --tags --always --dirty 2>/dev/null || echo dev)\nLDFLAGS := -s -w -X main.version=$(VERSION)\n\n.PHONY: build test lint fmt clean cve-fixtures chart-test image-test\n\nbuild:\n\tCGO_ENABLED=0 go build -ldflags \"$(LDFLAGS)\" -o bin/ard ./cmd/ard\n\ntest:\n\tgo test ./...\n\nlint:\n\tgo vet ./...\n\t@out=\"$$(gofmt -l .)\"; if [ -n \"$$out\" ]; then echo \"gofmt needed:\"; echo \"$$out\"; exit 1; fi\n\nfmt:\n\tgofmt -w .\n\n# Renders the two Argo CD charts under deploy/ and checks them (needs helm and yq;\n# run by the ard-ci pipeline, not part of `make test`).\nchart-test:\n\tdeploy/test-charts.sh\n\n# Builds the ard image for both platforms and checks it (needs Docker buildx and\n# network; not part of `make test`).\nimage-test:\n\tdeploy/test-image.sh\n\n# Regenerates the real cve scanner fixtures inside the pinned tool image.\n# Needs Docker and network; not part of `make test`.\ncve-fixtures:\n\tdocker build -t ard-cve-fixtures internal/roles/scanners/cve\n\tdocker run --rm --user \"$$(id -u):$$(id -g)\" -e HOME=/tmp \\\n\t  -v \"$$PWD/internal/roles:/roles\" -w /roles ard-cve-fixtures sh testdata/cve/regen.sh\n\nclean:\n\trm -rf bin\n66:   ./bin/ard serve --repo albertcbrown/ard-scratch 2>&1 | tee -a ~/.ard/gate2-scratch.log\n69:   `serve` creates its bare clone under `~/.ard/repos/` on first start.\n83:   **Squash and merge**. Stop `serve` with Ctrl-C (SIGINT); it stops cleanly\n88:signal, restarting `serve` when no job is running (`SELECT COUNT(*) FROM jobs\n132:4. **Run** `serve` as in stage A with `--repo albertcbrown/ard`, in tmux or\n85:Four more labels sit outside the grammar: `ard:approved` (human go-ahead before a worker starts; counts only when a triage-or-higher collaborator added it after triage finished), `ard:tests:failed` (worker could not get tests green; set by worker close-out on the issue and the PR, cleared by a green run), `ard:keep` (hold failed-job resources past 24 h) and `ard:pause` (on the pinned status issue, stops all new claims). `ard labels sync` creates and updates the full set from the resolved config, idempotently, and never deletes a label: stale `ard:` labels are only reported. In `label` mode a custom `intake.label` is part of the catalogue: sync creates it with a description saying it admits the issue to triage, and switching back to the default leaves the old custom label reported as stale, never deleted. `ard labels sync` and `ard config check` reject an intake label that is one of ard's own label names, naming `intake.label`; `ard serve` logs `config.intake_invalid` once per distinct value. `--repo owner/name` names the repository; when omitted, it is read from the `origin` remote. Role names are at most 31 characters and scanner names at most 38, because GitHub caps a label at 50 characters. Worker role names may not contain an all-digit segment (`eng-12`), so branch names stay unambiguous.\n184:**Each scanner has its own state, and GitHub is the truth.** Every scanner keeps a separate SQLite file, `~/.ard/state/<owner>/<repo>/<scanner>.db`: fingerprint to issue, first and last seen, suppressions, last run. Scanners never share or lock each other's state, and one can be reset without touching the rest. The controller keeps its own jobs, attempts, events and a small key/value table (for example the status issue number) in `_controller.db` in the same directory, and dry-run uses `~/.ard/state-dryrun/<owner>/<repo>/`. Owner and repo are lowercased (GitHub names are case-insensitive) and nested, so distinct repos never share a directory (`a-b/c` and `a/b-c` would collide as `a-b-c`); `ard serve` and `ard rebuild-state` refuse to start while a pre-#82 flat `<owner>-<repo>` state directory exists under `state/` or `state-dryrun/`, and ard does not migrate it (decided, #82). Every issue body carries hidden markers, so `ard rebuild-state <scanner>` restores a database from GitHub after a lost disk or a host move. The body holds a header `<!-- ard:scanner=<name> group=<enc> -->` and a checklist between `<!-- ard:findings -->` and `<!-- /ard:findings -->`, one line per finding: `- [ ] <title> <!-- ard:fp=<enc> sev=<severity> [reg=<old issue>] -->`. `<enc>` percent-encodes every byte outside `[A-Za-z0-9._:/@+=,~-]`, so the proposal's example fingerprint stays readable and nothing can close the comment. A ticked box means the finding is gone. Per-finding details sit in a second span, `<!-- ard:details -->`, cut to 2,000 characters each and trimmed from the end to keep the body under 60,000; `issue.md` holds them untruncated. The controller reads and rewrites only the two spans, so text a human or triage adds elsewhere survives. A human who deletes a marker line makes that finding look new, which is accepted because GitHub is the truth.\n186:`ard rebuild-state <scanner>` takes the leader lock (it refuses to run while `ard serve` holds it), reads every issue with `ard:scanner:<name>`, open and closed, and replaces the scanner's findings and suppressions in one transaction, keeping the run history. For each fingerprint, an open issue wins (the lowest number), else the highest-numbered closed issue decides: completed leaves a `fixed` tombstone, not planned or duplicate leaves none. Independently, the highest-numbered not-planned or duplicate close gives a suppression until `closed_at + stale.suppress_for`, kept even when expired. It prints what it adds, removes and changes; with `--dry-run` it prints and writes nothing. The dry-run state database holds synthetic issues that are not on GitHub, so it is never rebuilt. Only scanners with a `scan` executable can be rebuilt. One known gap: reopening an old issue that shares a fingerprint with a newer one can leave the database different from a rebuild until the next rebuild-state.\n254:Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands; fetches, commits and pushes do not take it. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.co